<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Appalachian Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Appalachians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Appalachian Mountains are an ancient mountain system of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, stretching about 2,400 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. For British colonists they formed a barrier to westward settlement, marked by the Proclamation Line of 1763, until settlers crossed into Kentucky and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> through passes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> Gap. The southern Appalachians were the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, and the region later became a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains.jpg" alt="Appalachian Mountains" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J Guth · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_Ridge_Parkway_Sunset_(43691100).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cherokee, in their own language Tsalagi, lived in towns across the southern <b>Appalachians</b>, in parts of present-day Georgia, Tennessee, North Carolina, South Carolina and Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When colonial settlers first crossed the <b>Appalachians</b> they were astonished by the earthworks.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Craftsmen hammered copper from the Great Lakes and the <b>Appalachians</b> into thin sheets and embossed them with figures such as the winged falcon dancers on the plates from Etowah, which are thought to have been made at Cahokia in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>(The Shakers had once worshipped on Holy Mount, in the Massachusetts part of the <b>Appalachians</b>.)</blockquote><a href="/songs/simple-gifts">Simple Gifts</a></li><li><blockquote>After Britain gained France's North American territories, King George III issued a proclamation reserving the lands west of the <b>Appalachians</b> for Indigenous nations and forbidding settlement there.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-7">7 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1784 North Carolina offered its western counties beyond the <b>Appalachians</b> to Congress.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-franklin">State of Franklin</a></li></ul></section>
